course: dictionary
lesson: 14966
title: Reluctance: not wanting to
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Reluctance is a noun, a word for a feeling. It means you do not want to do something, so you do it slowly or unwillingly. «With great reluctance, he handed over the ball.» He did not want to give it away, so he passed it over slowly and with a sour face.
explain: You can show reluctance in everyday life too. «Ben had a reluctance to go to bed» means he did not want to go, so he dragged his feet and asked for one more story. «She showed reluctance when asked to say sorry» means she did not want to say it.
explain: Look for clue words. Phrases like «with reluctance», «a reluctance to» and «show reluctance» all point to the same feeling. To check your meaning, swap in "unwillingness". If the sentence still makes sense, you have got it.
fact: Reluctance means not wanting to do something, so you do it slowly or unwillingly.
fact: You can say «with reluctance» or «a reluctance to», but you do not usually say «reluctances».
fact: Reluctance often shows in your body: slow feet, a long face, or a pause before you answer.
fact: In science, reluctance is a technical word: how much a material resists a magnetic field.
fact: Reluctance is the opposite of eagerness, which means wanting to do something very much.
exercise: «With great reluctance, he handed over the ball.» What does reluctance mean here? | Doing it quickly and happily | Shouting loudly | Being very tired | *Not wanting to, so doing it slowly
exercise: Mum asks Sam to tidy his room. He drags his feet and sighs. What is he showing? | Hunger | Excitement | Curiosity | *Reluctance
exercise: Find and copy the word in «She showed a reluctance to eat her vegetables» that names the feeling. | eat | showed | *reluctance | vegetables
test: «He agreed to help, but with obvious reluctance.» What does reluctance mean here? | Happy to be asked | *Not wanting to, so doing it unwillingly | Too tired to speak | Very excited about it
test: Which phrase shows reluctance? | a loud cheer after the goal | *a reluctance to go to bed | a great joy at the park | a quick, happy hug
exam: Jo says reluctance means being happy to do something. Is Jo right? | No, it means being very loud | Yes, it means being very tired | *No, it means not wanting to do it | Yes, it means being happy to do it
